titre $ Key to collybioid and marasmioide species- Gro Gulden
com$ Gro Gulden A preliminary guide to the macromycetes in the Finse area / GF 2011
1,20$ généralités
1,2 $ Pileus surface fine granulose, stipe surface similar up to a zone ca 0.5 cm from apex
1,3 $ Pileus and stipe not granulose
2,0 $ Pileus and stipe ochre to ochre-brown, spores amyloid, arthrospores under pileipellis present $ Cystoderma jasonis
2,0 $ Pileus and stipe rusty-brown, spores inamyloid, arthrospores lacking $ Cystoderma granulosum
3,4 $ Smell/taste farinaceous
3,5 $ Smell/taste otherwise or absent
4,0 $ Fruitbody blackish brown, carbonicolous $ Lyophyllum anthracophilum
4,0 $ Fruitbody paler, yellowish beige to horn-grey, not growing in burns $ Lyophyllum confusum
5,6 $ Stipe 3 mm thick or more
5,9 $ Stipe filiform or up to 3 mm thick
6,0 $ Stipe velutinous; lignicolous $ Flammulina velutipes
6,7 $ Stipe smooth; terricolous
7,0 $ Fruitbody white, often subfasciculate or in groups; flesh discolours violet FeSO4 $ Lyophyllum connatum
7,8 $ Fruitbody with yellow to deep red-brown colours
8,0 $ Pileus dark, red-brown-purplish brown $ Gymnopus alpinus
8,0 $ Pileus pale, yellow $ Gymnopus dryophilus
9,10 $ Stipe filiform
9,14 $ Stipe 1.5 -3 mm thick
10,11 $ Lamellae attached to a collarium around the stipe
10,12 $ No collarium
11,0 $ Lamellae few (5-9) and ± reduced; on herbal remains $ Marasmius limosus
11,0 $ Lamellae 10-20, normal; on small twigs $ Marasmius rotula
12,0 $ Fruitbody ± vinaceous red to purple-violet $ Pseudobaeospora pilodii
12,13 $ Fruitbody white to brown
13,0 $ Pileus and stipe whiteish, attached to a sclerotium $ Collybia tuberosa
13,0 $ Pileus brown, stipe blackish brown $ Marasmius androsaceus
14,15 $ Lamellae white
14,0 $ Lamellae brown, pigment green, eventually dissolving with KOH $ Gymnopus obscurus
15,0 $ Stipe dark brown tomented, growing on Dryas $ Marasmius epidryas
15,0 $ Stipe white pubescent, white tomentose at base $ Gymnopus terginus

20,0$In contrast to the species of the clitocybeoid and the omphalinoid genera, the lamellae are not decurrent in this group. However, two species of Lyophyllum may have somewhat decurrent lamellae. Regularly, the lamellae are adnexed or sometimes free or attached to a collarium. The species are tiny to fairly small, with pilei up to 3-5 cm wide at the most. Anatomically they show much variation for example in pileipellis structure and cystidia.///Genera included: Collybia, Cystoderma, Flammulina, Gymnopus, Lyophyllum, Marasmius iand Pseudobaeospora
